Multibyte conversions as C and POSIX have them, where Bionic's are not:
c32rtomb (and wcrtomb over it) makes no UTF-8 of a surrogate or of a value
past 0x10FFFF, which are no characters, but fails with EILSEQ; c16rtomb's
low surrogate with no high one before it, or a high one not followed by a
low one, is EILSEQ, an encoding error as C and POSIX say, not EINVAL;
mbrtoc16 stores the null character it reads, as C says ("which is the
value stored"); mbsnrtowcs stops at the end of its input on an incomplete
character, which its state keeps for the call with the rest, as
POSIX.1-2024 says, not EILSEQ.

